  "question": {
    "text": "The compound that does NOT liberate CO<sub>2</sub>, on treatment with aqueous sodium bicarbonate solution, is&nbsp;",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "yrR6a",
      "text": "Benzoic acid",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "XB16m",
      "text": "Benzenesulphonic acid",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "vXYUC",
      "text": "Salicylic acid",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "sT6tg",
      "text": "Carbolic acid (Phenol)",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Except phenol, all other options are either carboxylic acids or sulphonic acids, which are comparatively strong. Phenol is a very weak acid&nbsp;<math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>K</mtext> <mtext>a</mtext> </msub> <mo>&asymp;</mo> <mn>1</mn> <msup> <mn>0</mn> <mrow> <mn>-1</mn> <mn>0</mn> </mrow> </msup> </mrow> </math>&nbsp;and &nbsp;hence will not displace&nbsp;CO<sub>2</sub> from NaHCO<sub>3</sub>",
    "image": null
  },
